{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Hc4hzE33Z3ESUS3V3BbN22","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: \n- `cmd/<name>/` - One directory per utility with main.go and tests\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing framework\n- `pkg/sys/` - System call abstractions\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `pkg/hashutil/` - Hash computation utilities\n- `pkg/sizeparse/` - Size string parsing\n- `pkg/encutil/` - Encoding utilities\n- `docs/specs/` - SRDs, use cases, test suites\n- `magefiles/` - Build automation\n\n**Current State**: I'm checking the requirement_states to identify which SRDs have requirements ready for implementation, then scanning through the releases in order to find the earliest ones that still need work.\n\nLooking at the code status across releases, I can see that version 00.0 has partial completion with format and testutils use cases not yet started, while most subsequent releases through 03.0 are fully implemented. Starting from 03.1 onward, there's a pattern of partial completion—nproc in 03.1, ls in 04.0, and several utilities like echo, tac, and expand scattered across 05.0 and 05.1 that haven't been started yet. Looking at the remaining sections, most of the later releases haven't been started yet. Now I need to cross-reference the SRDs with their corresponding releases and use cases to identify which requirements are actually ready for implementation—srd004-ts maps to rel15.0, srd036-mktemp to rel06.0, and srd039-env and srd040-printenv to rel06.1, with varying levels of readiness across their R-items.\n\nContinuing through the mapping, I see that srd041 through srd058 all have their requirements fully ready across releases 06.1 and 07.0-07.1, covering utilities like id, whoami, groups, sort, tr, tail, cp, mv, and rm.\n\nNow I'm noticing a pattern: the requirement_states only shows SRDs with at least one pending requirement. SRDs that are completely implemented won't appear there, so I need to cross-reference against the code_status to find which ones might be fully done versus which haven't been tracked yet—like srd003-format and srd001-testutils that are absent from the requirements list. I see the constraint now—I can only propose tasks for requirements that are actually listed in requirement_states with a \"ready\" status. Everything else is either already complete or not being tracked, so I should ignore those. My actual scope of work is limited to the ready items across those tracked SRDs. Looking at the releases, rel06.0 has some ready requirements in srd036-mktemp, while rel06.1 and rel07.0 have multiple SRDs with all items ready, so I should prioritize based on which release comes first. Continuing through the releases, I'm seeing more SRDs mapped across rel07.1 through rel08.1, with file operations like cp, mv, and rm in the earlier batch, then utilities for date, sleep, touch, and various text processing tools filling out the later releases. Continuing through the utility commands, I'm mapping out the remaining tools across their release versions—mkfifo and mknod for process management in rel11.0, user-related commands like who and pinky in rel11.1, then moving into file operations like shred and install in rel12.0, with test and stty utilities distributed across later releases.\n\nNow I'm adding the final batch of utilities and noticing that rel06.0 with srd036-mktemp appears to be the earliest release with all its requirements ready. I need to verify the requirement weights for each task to ensure none exceed the limit of 4.\n\nSince I can't access the requirements.yaml file directly and the weights aren't provided in the project context, I'll proceed assuming each requirement has a default weight of 1 unless there's evidence suggesting otherwise.\n\nNow I need to stay within the 4-task limit and prioritize the most impactful work. Starting with the earliest incomplete release, rel06.0, I see that mktemp is the only utility with ready requirements—6 of them total. That breaks down into 2 tasks at 4 requirements per task. After that, I should move to rel06.1, which has several utilities waiting to be implemented: env with 12 requirements, printenv with 10, id with 13, whoami with 7, and groups with 9—all currently not ready. R1.3, R2.1 (weight: 4 × 1 = 4)\n4. **Task 3**: srd039-env R2.2, R2.3, R3.1, R3.2 (weight: 4 × 1 = 4)\n\nI need to account for the package contracts constraint—before implementing a new package, I should propose a contract task that defines all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs. Looking back at mktemp, the code_status shows \"not started\" but only 6 ready requirements remain, which suggests some requirements were already completed in an earlier phase.\n\nThere's a contradiction here: the code_status says \"not started\" yet requirement_states only lists R3.5, R3.6, and R4.1-R4.4 as ready, meaning R1.x through R3.4 aren't in ready state. If those earlier requirements were complete, that would explain the gap, but it conflicts with the \"not started\" status. Since I don't have source code information to verify whether mktemp already exists, I'll work with what the requirement_states tell me and propose tasks for the ready requirements.\n\nFor env, the code_status is \"not started\" and it's not in the package_contracts list, so I'd normally need a contract task first—but that would consume one of my four available tasks. Looking at the package_contracts, they only cover shared packages in pkg/, not the individual cmd/ utilities, which typically don't require separate contracts since they're standalone binaries.\n\nRe-reading the constraint more carefully, it specifies that contract tasks are needed for packages with exported types and interfaces. Since cmd/ packages are main packages with no exported types, the contract requirement doesn't apply to them. So I can move forward with implementation tasks for rel06.0 (mktemp with 6 ready requirements) and rel06.1 (env, printenv, id, whoami, groups with 51 ready requirements total), focusing on the earliest incomplete release.\n\nFor task sizing, I need to respect the constraint of at most 4 SRD sub-requirements per task with a total weight not exceeding 4. This means mktemp's 6 requirements will split into 2 tasks. Since I don't have direct access to the SRD content, I'll write the task descriptions with requirement IDs and point to the SRD for the stitch agent to read the details.\n\nI'm organizing 4 tasks: mktemp split across two tasks (R3.5-R4.2 in the first, R4.3-R4.4 in the second), and whoami as the simplest utility in rel06.1, also split into implementation and test tasks. Each task respects the weight and requirement count constraints. Looking at the naming patterns, R1.x likely covers core functionality, R2.x handles options and flags, while R3.x addresses error handling and edge cases. Since I need to split implementation from tests but don't have the actual requirement details, I'm inferring that the test task probably focuses on the differential testing setup rather than individual requirement validation.\n\nFor cmd/ utilities specifically, there aren't separate types or interfaces—just main.go and a test file. So I should treat all the R-items as implementation requirements, with the test task being to create differential tests that verify those requirements work correctly. This means I need to split each utility into an implementation task for the main logic and a separate test task for the differential test coverage.\n\nLooking at mktemp's requirements, I see that R1.x through R3.4 aren't listed in the requirement_states, which means they're already implemented. I only need to handle R3.5 through R4.4, so I can group these into fewer tasks rather than splitting them across multiple implementation chunks.\n\nThe P2 rule requires separating implementation and tests, but since I'm modifying existing files rather than creating new ones, I should still respect that boundary. Let me map out a more efficient allocation: I can handle mktemp's remaining requirements in two implementation tasks plus one test task, leaving room for whoami to get started. I'm running into the constraint that tests for all six remaining mktemp requirements would exceed the four-requirement limit per task. Let me split the test coverage across two tasks instead—one for the first batch of features and another for the second batch. test task tests functionality that maps to specific requirements. I could frame it as:\n- Test task covers the test verification of R3.5, R3.6, R4.1, R4.2 (4 test cases)\n- Another test task covers R4.3, R4.4 (2 test cases)\n\nBut then I'd need 4 mktemp tasks and have no room for anything else. That seems wrong for just 6 remaining requirements on one utility.\n\nActually, let me re-read P2 more carefully: \"Decompose each feature into up to three tasks in this order: (1) types and interfaces, (2) implementation, (3) tests. A single task must never both implement and test the same feature.\"\n\nSo for mktemp's remaining features:\n- Types/interfaces: not needed (already exists)\n- Implementation: the 6 remaining requirements\n- Tests: differential tests for those requirements\n\nNow I'm realizing the 4-requirement limit applies to both implementation and test tasks. So I could combine the implementation tasks into one covering R3.5, R3.6, R4.3, R4.4, then handle the remaining two requirements separately. But wait, that's still 4 tasks total for mktemp when I factor in the corresponding test tasks. qs. Then R4.1, R4.2 need another task.\n\nActually wait, the limit says \"at most 4 SRD sub-requirements\", not \"at most 4 requirements from a single group.\" Let me just work with the numbers.\n\n6 implementation requirements → need 2 impl tasks (4 + 2)\n6 test requirements → need 2 test tasks (4 + 2)\nTotal: 4 tasks\n\nWith deps:\n- Task 0: mktemp impl R3.5, R3.6, R4.1, R4.2 (dep: -1)\n- Task 1: mktemp impl R4.3, R4.4 (dep: 0)\n- Task 2: mktemp tests R3.5, R3.6, R4.1, R4.2 (dep: 0)\n- Task 3: mktemp tests R4.3, R4.4 (dep: 1)\n\nThe rule about not mixing implementation and testing in a single task is pretty absolute, so I have to follow it. That means all 4 of my task slots are consumed by mktemp's remaining requirements. Let me see if there's a way to combine the smaller tasks instead.\n\nActually, this layout is clean and respects all the constraints. The issue is that I don't have the actual requirement details from the SRD, so I can only reference the requirement IDs. The task descriptions need to be meaningful enough for the stitch agent to understand what's being asked, but without access to the full SRD content, I'm limited to pointing at those specific requirements.\n\nLet me draft the tasks with the SRD file as required reading. I'm guessing the filename follows the pattern `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd036-mktemp.yaml` based on what I've seen in other requirement states. The requirement groups likely map to sections in the SRD—R3.5-R3.6 are part of group 3, R4.1-R4.4 are group 4, and earlier groups are already complete.\n\nNow I need to respect the canonical ordering constraint: documentation before code, contracts before implementations, types before consumers, implementation before tests. So implementation tasks must come before test tasks. Task 0 handles R3.5-R4.2 implementation, task 1 handles R4.3-R4.4 implementation (depending on task 0 since they modify the same file), and the test tasks follow after their corresponding implementations.\n\nTask 2 tests R3.5-R4.2 from task 0, and task 3 tests R4.3-R4.4 from task 1. Tasks 1 and 2 can run in parallel after task 0 completes, while task 3 depends on task 1.\n\nNow I'm considering whether to combine the test tasks, keeping in mind the constraint of at most 4 SRD sub-requirements per task. I'm checking whether I can combine tasks more efficiently. The implementation requirements total 6 items, which exceeds the 4-requirement limit, so I can't merge those two tasks. I could split them as R3.5, R4.1, R4.2, R4.3 in one task and R3.6, R4.4 in another, keeping the test tasks aligned at 3-4 requirements each. I'm settling on 4 tasks for mktemp, but I need to verify whether the package already exists since the code_status shows \"not started\" while only 6 requirements are marked \"ready\" — the earlier requirements aren't listed in requirement_states at all. I'll account for both possibilities by using \"modify\" for the files and including existing files in required_reading, since the package might have been partially implemented in a previous run. I need to reconsider my approach here. The instructions are clear that if source code already exists for a package, I shouldn't propose recreating it—I should modify instead. Given the recent commits about mktemp being in progress, the package likely already has some implementation, so I'll use the modify action for these tasks. Without access to the actual SRD, I'll write task descriptions that reference the specific requirements and point to the documentation for details on what R3.5, R3.6, and R4.1-R4.4 entail. I'm realizing the core issue: I need specific SRD content to write meaningful task descriptions, but I can't access files or use tools. So I'll work with what I know about mktemp from the project context and structure the tasks to reference the SRD requirements by ID, letting the stitch agent fill in the details from the actual specification. I'll be as concrete as possible about what mktemp does—creating temporary files and directories with unique names—and organize the tasks around the requirement groups and constraints like the 4 sub-requirement limit and weight distribution. I see the constraint now—each SRD requirement maps to exactly one task, but when a requirement exceeds the line budget and gets split into implementation and test tasks, both tasks reference the same requirement. So for mktemp, if I split R3.5, R3.6, R4.1, R4.2 across impl and test, both tasks list those same requirements, just with different scopes. Task 2 can actually run in parallel with task 1 since it only tests the requirements from task 0, which are already complete. Task 3 would then depend on task 1 to ensure the later requirements are implemented before testing them.\n\nLooking at the constraint of 4 requirements per task, combining all 6 remaining requirements into a single test task exceeds the limit. I could split the implementation into two tasks with 3 requirements each and do the same for testing, but that still leaves me with 4 tasks total. Let me explore whether I can be more strategic about the grouping.\n\nI could try pairing implementation and testing for the smaller requirements, but the constraint explicitly forbids a single task from both implementing and testing the same feature. So that approach won't work. Four tasks appears to be the minimum viable arrangement.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the file structure—each command has a main.go for implementation and a corresponding _test.go file for tests. The stitch agent will modify different files depending on whether it's an implementation or test task, so I need to make sure the task descriptions account for that. The test tasks should include any necessary test setup like binary building and utility lookups, though some of that might already exist in the partial mktemp implementation. Let me start drafting the actual YAML configuration with clear task descriptions.\n\nRather than guessing at the specific requirements, I'll reference the SRD directly in the task descriptions so the stitch agent can pull